.TH RUNE 1 "2026" "rune" "Rune Manual"
.SH NAME
rune \- compile Standard ML '97 to Rune bytecode
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rune
.RI [ options ]
.IR file.sml " ..."
.br
.B rune \-i
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR text ]...
.RB [ \-\-no\-init ]
.RB [ \-\-color=\fIwhen\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-basic ]
.RI [ file.sml " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rune
compiles one or more Standard ML '97 source files into a single Rune bytecode
file, which
.BR runevm (1)
runs. The source files are elaborated as one program in the order given, after
the basis library named by
.BR \-\-lib .
.PP
The compiler itself has no built-in library location. The
.B rune
on your
.B PATH
is a wrapper script that supplies
.B \-\-lib
for the installed basis library; a
.B \-\-lib
on the command line comes later and therefore wins.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-i
As the first argument: start the REPL,
.BR rune\-repl ,
beside the wrapper, instead of compiling, with the arguments that follow;
it uses its init file and those files, and then reads declarations from the
standard input, with a line editor on a terminal.
.B \-q
leaves out the banner,
.B \-e
runs the declarations of its text and ends,
.B \-\-no\-init
skips the init file
.RI ( ~/.config/rune/init.sml ),
.B \-\-color
colours the REPL's output always, never or on a terminal (auto), and
.B \-\-basic
reads a line at a time, as on a pipe,
.BR \-\-unicode [= \fIENC\fP]
reads the files it uses as
.B rune
reads its inputs under it, and what is typed as UTF-8,
.B \-\-no\-unicode\-lints
leaves out the warnings of UTS #39 as it does for
.BR rune ;
.B \-\-engine
is the session for a front in another process, JSON-RPC 2.0 on the
descriptors 3 and 4
.RI ( docs/repl-protocol.md ),
and
.B \-\-dap
the debugger of an editor, the Debug Adapter Protocol on the standard
input and output
.RI ( docs/dap.md ).
The wrapper does this, and the compiler never sees the option.
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE"
Write the bytecode to
.IR FILE .
The default is the first input with its
.B .sml
suffix replaced by
.BR .rbc .
.TP
.BI \-\-lib " DIR"
Take the basis library from
.IR DIR /basis ,
which must contain a
.B library.build
listing the source files to compile before the inputs. Required unless
.B \-\-no\-prelude
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-library " NAME"
Compile the library
.IR DIR / NAME
too, after the basis library and before the inputs:
.I NAME
is a directory under the
.B \-\-lib
directory, such as
.BR test/property ,
else under a root that the workspace's
.I rune.workspace
names (docs/build.md, The workspace),
or a path when it begins with
.B /
or
.BR . .
Its
.B library.build
has the basis library's form, and names the libraries it is written on,
which are compiled before it. A
library's files are chosen as the basis library's are, and may not use
.BR _prim .
.IB NAME # VIEW
sees that view of a library with a header (docs/build.md, A library's header).
.I NAME
.BI ( "structure A " as " B" , " ..." )
renames what it imports, as a build file's
.B libraries
does: two imports of one name in one scope, two libraries' or a library's
and the basis library's, are an error that a rename answers
(docs/build.md, Clashes).
Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prelude
Do not compile the basis library before the inputs. The result then has no
top-level environment at all, so it is only useful together with
.BR \-\-allow\-prim .
.TP
.BI \-\-basis " MODE"
With
.B demand
(the default) the compiler reads from the syntax of the inputs the names they
use, not every identifier, and compiles the files of the basis library that
provide a structure, signature or functor among them, the files those
require, and the few that every program gets.
With
.B all
it compiles every file. The library's index, which the compiler derives
from its build file and its sources and keeps in its store, records what
each file provides and requires.
.TP
.BI \-\-basis\-view " NAME"
See the basis library through its view
.IR NAME :
its seal files marked
.BI view= NAME\fR,\fP
which bind structures again under narrower signatures, are loaded after the
libraries, which see the basis library whole, so that only the program is
held to the view. A build file's
.B view
.I NAME
says the same (docs/build.md, Views of the Basis Library).
.TP
.B \-\-basis\-deps
Print the library files that the inputs load, one per line, and exit: a
file of the basis library by its name, one of another library by its path.
.TP
.B \-\-basis\-check
Check what the basis library's index says each file provides and requires
against the library sources, and exit; with
.BR \-\-library ,
those of the libraries too, whose requires column names what they use of the
basis library.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-prim
Allow the
.B _prim
extension in the inputs. It names a VM primitive directly and is how the basis
library is written; it is unchecked and not part of the language.
.TP
.B \-\-or\-patterns
Allow or-patterns, an extension of Successor ML and SML/NJ: a parenthesized
pattern
.RI ( p1 " | " p2 " | ...)"
matches what any of its alternatives matches. Every alternative binds the
same variables at the same types. Without the option the compiler takes
Standard ML '97 only.
.TP
.BR \-\-unicode [= \fIENC\fP]
Read the input files as Unicode text in the encoding
.IR ENC :
.B utf8
(the default) or
.BR latin1 .
Without it they are read in the default mode, which the Basis Library and
the libraries beside it are always read in. The encoding is taken only
after an
.BR = :
.B "\-\-unicode latin1"
names an input file
.BR latin1 .
A build file's annotation
.RB ( ascii ,
.BR unicode ,
.BR unicode=latin1 )
gives a unit's mode over the command line's, and the cache keeps a unit
read in one mode apart from one read in another
.RI ( docs/units.md ,
Source modes). By default a literal holds ASCII alone, and
.B \eU
is refused; under
.B \-\-unicode
a raw character above 127 in a literal means its code point, which only
.B WideChar
and
.B WideString
take; under
.B \-\-unicode=latin1
the file is Latin-1, made UTF-8 as it is read, and a raw character is
its own byte
.RI ( docs/language.md ,
Strings).
Under either an alphanumeric identifier may hold the letters, marks and
digits of the identifier profile of
.IR lib/unicode ,
in NFC
.RI ( docs/language.md ,
Identifiers).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-unicode\-lints
Do not warn of what UTS #39 finds in the names of a source read under
.BR \-\-unicode :
a character it restricts, a name that mixes scripts, a name that looks
like another, like a reserved word or like punctuation; nor of a
lookalike of
.B *)
in a comment (UTS #55), in every mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-default\-type= T
In the input files, not the libraries, make an integer or word constant, or an
overloaded operator of integers or words, that its declaration leaves open
default to
.B Int64.int
.RI ( T " = " int64 )
or
.B Word64.word
.RI ( T " = " word64 )
instead of
.B int
and
.BR word ,
as MLton's
.B \-default\-type
does;
.I int
and
.I word
set the default back. The option may be given once for each.
.TP
.BI \-\-flex\-records= MODE
What must determine the labels of a flexible record
.RB ( {a,\ ...} ,
.BR #a ),
which the Definition leaves open:
.B binding
(the binding that would generalise it, as SML/NJ),
.B declaration
(the declaration at structure level it is in, as MLKit),
.B topdec\-monomorphic
(the top-level declaration, to a
.B ;
or the end of the file, none generalised, as Moscow ML),
.B topdec
(the default, as Poly/ML and MLton: the same, one generalised determined at
each use) or
.B program
(the whole program, or the unit).
.TP
.BI \-\-overloading= MODE
What must determine the type of an overloaded operator or constant
.RB ( + ,
.BR 1 )
before it defaults
.RB ( int ,
.BR real ,
\&...), which the Definition bounds by the smallest enclosing declaration at
structure level:
.B binding
(the innermost value declaration, as HaMLet),
.B declaration
(the default: the declaration at structure level it is in, the Definition's
bound, as SML/NJ and MLKit),
.B toplevel
(the declaration at top level, a whole structure, local or functor, as Moscow
ML and Rune before the option) or
.B topdec
(the top-level declaration, to a
.B ;
or the end of the file, as Poly/ML and MLton). A functor's body is settled
where it ends in every mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-free\-tyvars= MODE
What becomes of a type variable that a top-level declaration leaves free
.RB ( "val r = ref []" ),
which the Definition refuses or makes a monotype:
.B refuse
(the default, as HaMLet: an error, where the top-level declaration ends, to a
.B ;
or the end of the file),
.B monotype
(a new type there, with a warning, as Poly/ML),
.B dummy
(a new type where each value declaration at structure level ends, as SML/NJ),
.B later
(what any later use in the program, or the unit, makes of it, an error where
none does, as MLKit and Moscow ML) or
.B later\-monotype
(the same, a new type where none does, as MLton).
.TP
.BI \-\-functor\-budget= N
The nodes of the middle end the copies of a top-level application of a
functor, and of the applications in them, may hold (1000000 by default;
0 makes none). Each functor is compiled once, to a function; a whole
program, and a unit's own applications, are given copies of its body back
within this budget, and any other application calls the function
.RI ( docs/ir.md ,
Functors compiled once).
The options that chose this until then,
.B \-\-functors=
and
.BR \-\-datatype\-tags= ,
are gone.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-inline\-functors
Inline across units none of the functors and functions the libraries'
build files mark
.RB ( "inline functor" ,
.BR "inline val" ;
.IR docs/build.md ).
A program marks its own in its build file.
.TP
.B \-\-units
Compile every file, the basis library's too, as a unit of its own after the
files before it, and link the units into the program
.RB ( docs/units.md ).
The program behaves as the one compiled whole does; no function of one unit
is inlined into another. Each unit compiled is kept in a cache and taken
from there by the compiles after it while nothing it was compiled from
changes.
.TP
.BI \-\-emit\-units " DIR"
With
.BR \-\-units ,
write each unit's code
.RI ( .rbu )
and basis
.RI ( .rbi )
into
.I DIR
too, and
.IR DIR /units,
their names in order;
.B rune \-o
.I P.rbc
given those files links them.
.TP
.BI \-\-cache\-dir " DIR"
With
.BR \-\-units ,
keep the cache of units, the store, in
.IR DIR .
The default is
.BR $RUNE_CACHE ,
else
.B .rune
in the workspace, the directory of the nearest
.I rune.workspace
above the build file or the first input, else beside that file. A compile
reads the frozen libraries,
.IR <libdir>/cache ,
after it, and never writes them.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-cache
With
.BR \-\-units ,
compile every unit, and keep none.
.TP
.B \-\-cache\-trim
Remove from the store what no compile took for longer than
.B \-\-cache\-max\-age
allows, then, the least recently taken first, what makes it larger than
.B \-\-cache\-max\-size
allows, and do nothing else. A compile in units does the same after its
work, where the store was last trimmed a day ago or more. What a compile
does not find in the store, it compiles.
.TP
.BI \-\-cache\-max\-age " DAYS"
What
.B \-\-cache\-trim
keeps of what no compile took: a whole number of days, 7 unless given, or
.BR none .
.TP
.BI \-\-cache\-max\-size " SIZE"
How large
.B \-\-cache\-trim
leaves the store: bytes, or a number with
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.B G
or
.B T
after it, 4G unless given, or
.BR none .
.TP
.B \-\-explain\-rebuild
With
.BR \-\-units ,
say of each unit on standard error whether it was taken from the cache, or
why it was compiled.
.TP
.BI \-j " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-jobs " N"
With
.B \-\-units
and the cache, compile the units in up to
.I N
processes at once, each unit after those it uses; the program is the
bytes of a build by one process.
.TP
.B \-\-serve
Answer requests on standard input, one JSON-RPC 2.0 message a line: the
diagnostics of an editor's buffer, and the type of the variable at an
offset of it, each compiled by a child process with the other options
given. A prototype of the compiler as a service.
.TP
.B \-\-verify\-cache
With
.BR \-\-units ,
compile each unit the cache has too, and fail where the cache's is not
the same bytes as what compiling it gives, or its schemes of what it names
of other units are not.
.B $RUNE_VERIFY_CACHE
set and not empty does the same, and for the REPL's
.B use
too;
.BI $RUNE_VERIFY_DUMP= DIR
writes each entry that does not verify, and what compiling it gave, to
.IR DIR .
.TP
.B \-\-deps
Print the inputs in an order where each comes after the files that declare
the modules it uses, each read in the fixity the files before it leave, or
name a cycle. A file that is not a well-formed unit, one others depend on
that binds a value, an exception, a datatype or a fixity at its top level,
opens a structure there, or declares a module another of its scope declares,
stays where it is written, with a warning for each break (docs/units.md,
Well-formed units). With
.BR \-\-build ,
the build's files in its order, read as the build reads them, its preludes
and its scopes the build's.
.TP
.B \-\-deps\-dot
The same, as a graph in DOT.
.TP
.BI \-I " DIR"
With one input, a program's entry point: compile before it the files of its
directory and of each
.I DIR
that declare the modules it uses, and those they need.
.TP
.BI \-\-build " FILE"
Compile in units what the build file
.I FILE
says: its files, each in the basis the declarations before it leave, with
the export lists of the libraries it uses and its virtual modules'
implementations (docs/build.md). The steps it declares run first, each
where something it reads has changed, and its preprocessors make what their
files compile; a build that is up to date runs none (docs/build.md,
Generated sources).
.TP
.BI \-\-build\-list " FILE"
Print the files of the build file
.I FILE
and of the libraries it names, with those they are written on, in the order
they are compiled.
.TP
.B \-\-plan
Print the plan of what the rest of the command line says: of a build file
.IR FILE .build
given as the one input, or of the files and libraries given. The plan is
line-oriented text that needs no Rune to read: the files in compile order, the
libraries with their boundaries, and the build files it was made from, each
with its SHA-256 (docs/build.md, The plan). A step is printed and not run.
Given a
.IR FILE .plan,
read it and print it again.
.TP
.BI \-\-path " NAME" = DIR
The path variable
.BI $( NAME )
of build files is
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BI \-\-basis\-index " DIR"
Print the index of the library in
.IR DIR ,
what each of its files provides and requires, made again from the
library's build file
.IB DIR /library.build
and its sources, with what a client of the library cannot act on said as a
warning. A compile derives the index itself, and keeps it in its store.
.TP
.B \-\-whole
With
.BR \-\-units ,
make one program of the units' Mid, kept in the cache, with the passes of a
whole-program compile, rather than link the units' code: the same bytes as
compiling the program whole.
.TP
.B \-\-typecheck\-only
Stop after type checking; write no bytecode.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-warnings
Do not print warnings about nonexhaustive or redundant matches.
.TP
.BI \-\-color= WHEN
Colour the diagnostics:
.BR always ,
.B never
or
.BR auto ,
the default, which colours them when the standard error is a terminal (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
.TP
.BI \-\-diagnostics= FORM
.B full
shows the lines of source a diagnostic is about under its first line, with
carets under the span;
.B short
shows the first line only,
.IR file : line : col ": error: " message ,
as earlier versions did. The default is full when the standard error is a
terminal and
.B TERM
is not
.BR dumb .
.TP
.BI \-\-diagnostics\-json " FILE"
Also write every diagnostic of the run to
.I FILE
as JSON, in the form rune-ide reads (docs/diagnostics.md).
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-tokens
Print the tokens of each input and stop: no library is loaded and nothing is
compiled or written.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-ast
Print the parsed program.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-lambda
Print the intermediate representation; the same as
.BR \-\-dump\-after=translate .
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-code
Print the generated bytecode; the same as
.B \-\-dump\-after=registers
.RB ( stack
with
.BR \-\-target=stack ).
.TP
.BR \-O0 ", " \-O1 ", " \-O2
The optimisation level; \-O1 when none is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-passes= P,...
Run these optional passes, whatever the level.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-before= P
Print what the pass
.I P
is given, or every pass for
.BR all .
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-after= P
Print what the pass
.I P
makes, or every pass for
.BR all ;
variables are numbered afresh, in the order they appear.
.TP
.B \-\-lint
Check what every pass makes, and stop at the first breach of what its
representation keeps: a bug of the compiler.
.TP
.BI \-\-fuel= N
Make no more than
.I N
rewrites in the passes that optimise; halving it finds the rewrite that breaks
a program.
.TP
.B \-\-pass\-stats
Print the size of what every pass makes and the time it took, on standard
error.
.TP
.BI \-\-read\-mid " file"
Read a program of the intermediate representation Mid, in the text
.B \-\-dump\-after=mid
prints, and make and check it as the pass
.B mid
would; no bytecode is written.
.TP
.B \-\-mid\-roundtrip
Check that Mid printed with its positions, read and printed again is the same.
.TP
.BI \-\-target= T
The bytecode to make:
.B registers
for
.BR runevm ,
the default, or
.B stack
for
.BR runevm\-stack .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set and not empty, no colours: on a terminal the diagnostics keep their
bold and underline.
.TP
.BR FORCE_COLOR ", " CLICOLOR_FORCE
When set to anything but empty,
.B 0
or
.BR false ,
colour also where the standard error is not a terminal.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR
.B 0
turns colour off.
.TP
.B TERM
.BR dumb ,
or unset on a system other than Windows, turns colour off and makes the
diagnostics short.
.TP
.B RUNE_COLORS
A theme
.RB ( auto ", " dark ", " light ", " mono ", " none )
and styles of roles, separated by colons, as in
.BR dark:error=1;38;5;203:diag.gutter=2 ;
a style is SGR parameters (docs/diagnostics.md).
.TP
.B COLORFGBG
Where the terminal sets it, its background chooses the theme
.B dark
or
.BR light .
.TP
.B RUNE_WARM
.B off
turns off the warm path of
.BR \-\-units :
every unit taken from the cache has its basis loaded, every source of a
unit is read for its key, and every build links and writes its output,
though nothing it rests on changed (docs/units.md, A warm build).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B rune
exits 0 when it wrote the bytecode (or when
.B \-\-typecheck\-only
found no errors) and 1 on a usage, compile or I/O error. Errors are reported on
standard error as
.IR file : line . column ": " message .
.SH EXAMPLES
Compile and run a program:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rune hello.sml \-o hello.rbc
runevm hello.rbc
.RE
.fi
.PP
Use a basis library from a build tree instead of the installed one:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rune \-\-lib ~/rune/lib hello.sml
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <libdir>/basis/library.build
The ordered list of basis library sources compiled before the inputs.
.TP
.I rune.workspace
The root of a workspace, the nearest above a build file: the directories its
libraries are found under, and its named targets. A build's units are named
from it.
.TP
.I .rune
The workspace's store of compiled units, beside its
.IR rune.workspace ,
which every build of the workspace, the REPL's
.B use
and the memos of a warm build share
.RB ( docs/units.md ,
The store).
.TP
.I <libdir>/cache
The frozen libraries: the Basis Library and the compiler's libraries,
compiled once, which every compile in units reads after its store, and the
index of every library beside them, which every compile reads so. No
compile writes them.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR runevm (1),
.BR runevm\-stack (1)
